ARTICLES = [
{"slug":"italy-2027-budget-law-one-billion-gambling-contribution-concessions-audit",
"title":"Italy's €1bn 2027 Budget Ask of Gambling Operators",
"category":"Tax",
"excerpt":"Rome reportedly wants €1bn from gambling in the 2027 Budget Law, with audits, concession charges and a possible early tender on the table.",
"publish_date":"2026-10-27T06:10:00Z",
"related_jurisdictions":["italy"],
"related_firms":["cms-italy","dla-piper-italy","norton-rose-fulbright-italy"],
"body":[
"Italy's Ministry of Economy and Finance (MEF) is reported to be looking to the gambling sector for roughly €1bn in the 2027 Budget Law, as part of a wider revenue target of around €7bn to €8bn that would also draw on banks, insurers and energy companies. The budget bill is expected to reach the Chamber of Deputies toward the end of October, and trade reporting stresses that neither final rates nor an implementation timetable has been confirmed. For operators and their advisers, the practical task is to separate what is genuinely proposed from what is speculation, and to build scenarios rather than commit to a single outcome.",
"The ideas reported so far fall into several buckets. On online gambling, the discussion centres on new auditing requirements for online concessions and player accounts, together with possible changes to licensing and technical standards. These would sit on top of the online regime that took effect in November 2025, which carries an annual €7m fee. Concessionaires should therefore expect questions about how audit obligations would interact with existing reporting duties to the Customs and Monopolies Agency (ADM), and whether a new audit layer would be performed by ADM, by accredited third parties, or by the operators themselves.",
"A second bucket concerns winnings and withdrawals. Reports mention possible revised rules on how winnings and withdrawals are accounted for, and new revenue streams tied to withdrawal transactions. From a legal standpoint, any charge tied to withdrawals raises design questions that Italian counsel will recognise: whether it is characterised as a tax or a fee, who bears legal incidence, whether it can be passed to players under consumer rules, and how it is reconciled with the existing gross gaming revenue and turnover-based levies. A poorly drafted measure could also create product-mix distortions between casino, poker and betting verticals.",
"Concession economics form a third bucket. The MEF is reported to be considering higher charges for extending existing concessions, and a proposal still awaiting parliamentary approval would let ADM start the instant-win and scratchcard tender a year early, ahead of the 2028 expiry, targeting a contract worth about €1bn. The incumbent is the Lotterie Nazionali joint venture. For bidders and incumbents alike, an accelerated tender compresses preparation time, and governance questions about consortium structure, financial standing and change-of-control approvals become urgent sooner than planned.",
"Retail is the fourth bucket. Retail gambling concessions are due to expire at the end of 2026, and the MEF is reportedly weighing a three-year extension to the end of 2029, which would keep licence-fee revenue flowing while the long-delayed retail reform remains stalled. Reports indicate that Prime Minister Giorgia Meloni ended talks with regional authorities in August and removed the reform from the 2027 legislative agenda. Land-based operators should treat an extension as a bridge rather than a settlement, and should test their contracts, leases and financing covenants against a 2029 horizon.",
"For compliance teams, the sensible course is to map each reported measure to the specific contract, system or reporting process it would touch. Finance functions should model margin sensitivity under alternative levy designs, while legal teams should review pass-through rights with affiliates, payment partners and B2B suppliers. Where concession extensions are charged, the timing of payment and any conditions attached, including exit or transfer provisions, deserve early review.",
"The broader lesson is that Italy, like several European markets this autumn, is treating gambling as a fiscal lever as well as a regulated activity. Operators that engage constructively with ADM and the MEF during the parliamentary window, and that document the channelisation and consumer-protection benefits of the existing regime, will be better placed to influence the final text. Specialist Italian counsel can help assess whether proposals are compatible with EU free-movement principles and with legitimate expectations under existing concession terms.",
"Because the bill is still moving, this analysis is based on reported proposals rather than enacted law. Readers should confirm the final text once published and should not treat any figure above as settled."
]},
{"slug":"netherlands-ksa-groothuizen-big-tech-dsa-enforcement-limits-illegal-ads",
"title":"KSA's 'Seduce or Fight' Stance on Big Tech and Illegal Ads",
"category":"Enforcement",
"excerpt":"KSA chair says fining platforms is slow and the DSA call sits with Brussels, so cooperation comes first and litigation waits.",
"publish_date":"2026-10-27T07:25:00Z",
"related_jurisdictions":["netherlands"],
"related_firms":["kalff-katz-and-franssen","stibbe","akd-benelux-lawyers"],
"body":[
"On 14 October 2026 the chairman of the Netherlands Gambling Authority (Kansspelautoriteit, KSA), Michel Groothuizen, published a blog post titled 'Verleiden of vechten? Of allebei?' ('Seduce or fight? Or both?'). It is one of the clearest public statements yet on how the regulator intends to deal with very large online platforms that carry advertising for unlicensed gambling. The message is pragmatic: pursue voluntary cooperation now, keep formal enforcement available, and be candid about the limits of the KSA's own powers.",
"The starting point is the channelisation problem. According to the post, around 90% of Dutch online gamblers play only with legal operators, yet measured by money staked, roughly one euro still flows to the illegal circuit for every euro gambled legally. Fines on illegal providers have, in the regulator's own assessment, had little practical effect on collection, so for the past 18 months the KSA has concentrated on making illegal operators harder to find and on cutting off the payments that reach them. Platforms that serve advertising are an obvious next target.",
"Political pressure to act is real. In a September 2026 parliamentary debate, VVD member Bart Bikkers urged the KSA to fine very large online platforms (VLOPs), and practitioner Justin Franssen has also called for firmer action. Groothuizen's reply is that fining technology companies is harder and slower than it looks, and that a failed case has consequences. His image of a sheriff who either misses or turns out to be firing a cap gun captures the concern that a weak first case would hand illegal operators a free pass.",
"The legal analysis in the post matters for operators and platforms alike. The KSA says it holds enough evidence that platforms facilitate prohibited gambling advertising, including advertising that reaches excluded players and minors, in volumes running to thousands of instances or more. Both Dutch law and the EU Digital Services Act (DSA) provide grounds for action. But under the DSA, enforcement against VLOPs is for the European Commission, and the KSA can supply grounds rather than decide. National litigation remains possible, though the post estimates proceedings could take about five years against well-resourced opponents who would contest every point.",
"That helps explain the cooperative track. The KSA and other European regulators met Google and Meta in Dublin in spring 2026, and a joint task force with Google is reported to be building a white list of legal EU operators, with the prospect that Google could block advertisers who are not on it. Groothuizen hopes this closes one main tap, while acknowledging that others remain. For licensed operators, a white list brings both benefit and obligation: listing could improve advertising reach, but it will depend on accurate licence data and on keeping every market licence in good standing.",
"What should counsel advise? Licensed operators should audit their own advertising supply chains, including affiliates and programmatic buyers, so that no licensed brand appears alongside or is mistaken for an unlicensed one. They should also prepare for white-list verification by assembling licence evidence in a form platforms can check quickly. Platforms and ad-tech intermediaries, for their part, should treat the KSA's statement that it has sufficient evidence as a signal that notice-and-action processes for gambling advertising will be scrutinised.",
"The post ends with a warning: if voluntary agreements prove insufficient, the KSA says it will act legally against a platform, and it invites the public to hold it to that. Whether that action would be national, European or both is unresolved, and the DSA's division of competence means the Commission's appetite will be decisive.",
"For now, the Dutch approach is a useful reference for other regulators facing the same structural problem: a national gambling authority with strong domestic grounds but limited leverage over global platforms. Cross-border operators should expect similar cooperation-first strategies elsewhere in Europe, backed by a credible, if distant, threat of formal proceedings."
]},
{"slug":"ontario-igaming-ontario-shelved-problem-gambling-study-privacy-commissioner-2026",
"title":"iGaming Ontario's Shelved Study: Transparency Questions",
"category":"Regulatory",
"excerpt":"Reports say iGaming Ontario withheld a problem gambling study; a May 2026 privacy ruling found a deemed refusal of an access request.",
"publish_date":"2026-10-27T08:40:00Z",
"related_jurisdictions":["canada"],
"related_firms":["joelson-llp","northridge-law-llp"],
"body":[
"A report published on 21 October 2026 alleges that iGaming Ontario, the provincial agency that conducts and manages Ontario's regulated online gambling market, shelved a problem gambling prevalence study after researchers reported rates significantly higher than expected. The agency, for its part, is quoted as saying the research lacked the rigour needed to guide policy. The allegations are contested and the full study has not been released, but the episode raises legal and policy questions that Ontario operators, advisers and public-law specialists should follow closely.",
"According to the report, the study was commissioned by iGaming Ontario and followed the same group of people across three waves over two years. The first data collection took place in February 2022, shortly before the market launched in April 2022. The work was led by researchers Iris Balodis and Michael Wohl and was cleared by Carleton University's Research Ethics Board, and their participation is said to have been conditional on publication. The report states that executives at iGaming Ontario decided to keep the results confidential, citing potential harm to the province's economic interests.",
"The documentary trail described in the report includes a December 2022 email asking a colleague not to share the findings widely, and a 2023 decision to hire a public relations firm for messaging connected with the study. These details are allegations drawn from records, and readers should treat them as such. What matters for regulatory analysis is the governance question they raise: how should a body that both conducts and manages a commercial market handle research that bears directly on the harms of that market?",
"There is also a freedom-of-information dimension. The Information and Privacy Commissioner of Ontario is reported to have found iGaming Ontario in deemed refusal of an access request on 14 May 2026, meaning the agency had not responded within the statutory period. A deemed refusal is a procedural finding rather than a ruling on the merits of any exemption, so the substantive question of whether economic-interest or other exemptions justify withholding the study remains open.",
"For operators registered with iGaming Ontario, the practical consequences are indirect but real. Public confidence in the regulatory model affects the political space for advertising rules, deposit limits, bonus restrictions and the centralised self-exclusion programme. If prevalence data in the shelved study show higher harm than assumed, pressure for tighter standards from the Alcohol and Gaming Commission of Ontario (AGCO) and the provincial government could increase. Operators that already invest in safer-gambling analytics may find it prudent to benchmark their own player-protection metrics against likely policy scenarios.",
"Counsel should also consider reputational and disclosure risk. Operators that cite the Ontario market's responsible-gambling credentials in investor or regulatory communications should ensure those statements remain accurate and balanced, and should avoid relying on prevalence assumptions that may be revisited. Where a business has funded or participated in research of its own, it should check contractual publication rights and data-sharing terms so it is not exposed to similar disputes.",
"More broadly, the episode illustrates a recurring tension in regulated markets between commercial stewardship and public-health transparency. Jurisdictions that separate market management, regulation and research funding tend to face fewer such challenges. Ontario's model combines a conduct-and-manage role at iGaming Ontario with AGCO as the regulator, and the debate may prompt calls for independent research governance.",
"Developments to watch include any release of the study, further decisions from the Information and Privacy Commissioner, parliamentary questions at Queen's Park, and any response from AGCO. Until then, operators should plan for the possibility of stricter harm-minimisation requirements and should keep documentation of their own compliance measures current."
]},
{"slug":"gibraltar-uk-remote-gaming-duty-economic-exposure-policy-debate-2026",
"title":"Gibraltar and UK Gaming Duty: Economic Exposure in Focus",
"category":"Tax",
"excerpt":"With UK duty changes on the books and political attention on the Rock, Gibraltar-based operators should stress-test their group structures.",
"publish_date":"2026-10-27T09:20:00Z",
"related_jurisdictions":["gibraltar","united-kingdom"],
"related_firms":["hassans-international-law-firm","isolas-llp","harris-hagan"],
"body":[
"Gibraltar's gaming industry has returned to the UK political conversation. Trade reporting on 26 October 2026 says Nigel Farage travelled to Gibraltar to hear about concerns over UK gambling policy. The visit follows a Commons debate on 11 March 2026 on the Finance Bill, in which Labour MP Gareth Snell warned that planned changes to remote gaming duty and remote betting duty could leave a huge hole in the Gibraltar economy. For licensed operators and their advisers, the combination of fiscal change and political attention warrants a structured review of exposure.",
"The economic facts cited in that debate are striking. Drawing on figures attributed to Gibraltar's Minister for Trade, Industry and Justice, Nigel Feetham, Mr Snell said gaming accounts for around 30% of Gibraltar's GDP, employs about 3,500 people and generates one third of tax receipts. His amendment calling for a UK impact assessment on Gibraltar was not adopted, and the Bill passed its third reading. The Exchequer Secretary, Dan Tomlinson, responded for the Government. Whatever one's view of the policy, the numbers explain why even modest changes in UK duty can have outsized consequences for the territory.",
"The legal mechanics are worth restating. UK point-of-consumption taxation applies by reference to where the customer is located, not where the operator is established. A Gibraltar-based operator serving UK customers therefore pays UK duty on that activity and must also hold a UK Gambling Commission licence. Gibraltar's own regime, under the Gambling Act 2025 and its secondary legislation, governs licensing, substance and supervision at home. Changes in UK duty rates therefore flow directly into operator margins without altering the Gibraltar licensing position.",
"For group structuring, the key question is where value is created and where costs are borne. Higher UK duty compresses margins on UK-facing products, which can prompt reviews of marketing spend, bonus design, product mix and headcount location. Operators with substantial Gibraltar workforces should model scenarios for restructuring, and should consider employment-law consequences under Gibraltar rules before decisions are taken. Contracts with B2B suppliers, affiliates and payment partners should be checked for volume-based commitments that could become uncomfortable if UK revenue falls.",
"Political risk is a separate dimension. Statements by visiting politicians do not change law, and a visit reported in the press is not a policy commitment. But positions taken on gambling taxation, advertising and affordability can shape future manifestos and regulatory priorities. Operators and trade bodies should therefore engage with all parties on facts, including contribution to employment, tax and safer-gambling investment, while being careful to comply with lobbying and political-donation rules in each jurisdiction.",
"Compliance considerations continue regardless of the fiscal debate. The UK Gambling Commission continues to pursue enforcement on anti-money-laundering and social-responsibility failings, and Gibraltar-based licensees remain within its reach through their UK licences. Recent settlements against operators for delayed withdrawals and AML failings illustrate that regulatory costs, not just tax, shape the economics of UK-facing business.",
"Practical steps for Gibraltar-based groups include updating financial models for alternative duty scenarios, reviewing transfer-pricing documentation for intragroup services, confirming that substance requirements in Gibraltar are met and evidenced, and preparing board papers that set out contingency plans. Legal advisers in both jurisdictions should coordinate, since tax, licensing and employment questions interact.",
"Because the situation is evolving, readers should check official sources, including HM Treasury, HMRC and the Gibraltar Government, for current rates and commencement dates before making commercial decisions."
]},
{"slug":"ukgc-chief-executive-search-acting-ceo-enforcement-continuity-operators-2026",
"title":"UKGC CEO Search: What Operators Should Expect Next",
"category":"Regulatory",
"excerpt":"With a new Chair, an acting CEO and a recruitment under way, the Gambling Commission's enforcement tempo is the question for licensees.",
"publish_date":"2026-10-27T10:30:00Z",
"related_jurisdictions":["united-kingdom"],
"related_firms":["harris-hagan","wiggin-llp","mishcon-de-reya-llp"],
"body":[
"The UK Gambling Commission (UKGC) is recruiting a new chief executive, according to trade reporting on 9 October 2026. Sarah Gardner is serving as acting Chief Executive, and on 21 October she delivered a keynote at the IAGR 2026 Conference. The Department for Culture, Media and Sport (DCMS) appointed Ruth Evans as the Commission's new Chair on 7 September, and a senior DCMS leader, Sarah Fox, was announced on 29 July as joining the Commission's Executive Team. Taken together, these changes mean the regulator's top team is in transition at a time when licensees face active enforcement.",
"For operators, the immediate question is whether leadership change alters enforcement priorities. The public record suggests continuity rather than pause. On 7 October the Commission announced that three nationwide casino operators would pay £5 million following anti-money-laundering and social responsibility failures. On 21 October it announced that L & L Europe would pay £900,000 for regulatory failures including delayed customer withdrawals. Licence suspensions have also continued, including that of Targetlocal Ltd on 21 September and of BresBet Ltd and Bet St George Ltd on 28 August.",
"The criminal side is equally active. On 23 October three people were sentenced for cheating on bets placed on the 2024 General Election, and on 10 September Anthony Lee pleaded guilty to two counts of cheating over the same bets. The Commission has also supported police operations against illegal gambling, including in Greater Manchester, Cardiff, Sheffield and Doncaster. This pattern shows an organisation that is using the full range of its powers, from regulatory settlements to prosecutions, irrespective of who sits in the chief executive's chair.",
"Still, a new chief executive will matter over a longer horizon. The appointee will inherit implementation of the reforms flowing from the 2023 White Paper, including statutory levy arrangements, affordability checks, machine standards and the evolving approach to gambling advertising. The Commission announced on 30 September that new standards were to be introduced to make gaming machines fairer and safer, signalling that product-level rules remain on the agenda. Operators will watch whether the new leader favours more prescriptive rules, more principles-based supervision, or a mix.",
"Licensees should use the transition period to strengthen the fundamentals that recent cases show regulators care about. Anti-money-laundering risk assessments should be current and tested against actual customer behaviour. Withdrawal processes should be examined for terms and practices that delay payments, since the L & L Europe settlement shows that consumer-protection concerns can lead to financial penalties. Social-responsibility triggers need to be tuned and evidenced, with clear records of interventions and outcomes.",
"Governance is another focus. Boards and senior managers who hold personal management licences should ensure that they receive timely information on compliance metrics and that decisions are minuted. Where suspensions have occurred, the common thread has often been the Commission's concern that a licensee could not demonstrate control over risk. A change at the top of the regulator is not a reason to relax internal assurance; if anything, new leadership tends to scrutinise how well operators have absorbed earlier lessons.",
"Engagement also matters. The Commission's consultations and speeches are the best guide to direction, and operators and trade associations can shape outcomes by responding with evidence. Specialist counsel can help frame submissions, prepare for compliance assessments and advise on voluntary settlement strategy where issues are identified.",
"Readers should note that recruitment details, including timing and candidate profile, have not been confirmed in the sources reviewed, and no appointment should be assumed. We will update this analysis when the Commission announces its decision."
]},
]
